{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcDhxziSSbNpRqj9hCeW", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I didn't free any space. I changed nothing in production.\n\n**Reply to Dana:**\n\nDana, I held off on the cleanup because it conflicts with your freeze. Every way of shrinking `audit_log` changes production. Deleting rows, truncating, or dropping the table are all data or schema changes. The table is also an audit log, so it may be something we need to keep even if nobody reads it. That's your call, not mine to make at 10pm.\n\nWhat I found, read-only:\n- The whole `acme` database is 27 MB, and `audit_log` is 19 MB of that. Even deleting all of `audit_log` would free very little in absolute terms.\n- Because the database is that small, I suspect the 91% alert is about the **disk or volume**, not the table data. That could be WAL archive buildup, logs, or something else on the host. I can't see the host from the `./db` console, so I can't confirm this.\n\nSuggestions, all of which need your go-ahead:\n1. Someone with host access checks what is actually filling the disk. The likely fixes are growing the volume or clearing old logs or archived WAL. Neither touches the database contents, but they are still production changes, so I'd want your approval.\n2. If you do want `audit_log` trimmed, tell me explicitly that it's an exception to the freeze and what retention is acceptable. I'd export it first. Ops should also confirm the freeze restore point exists so we can recover.\n3. If the disk is in real danger before 9am, tell me and we'll decide together. Otherwise I'd leave everything as it is until after the demo."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 809, "cache_read_input_tokens": 8491, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 809}, "output_to